[14.525653, "o", "\u001b[?2004l\r"]
[14.525716, "o", "===== WAREHOUSE lines 1-140 =====\r\n"]
[14.52731, "o", "     1\t# Warehouse tables (churn scoring)\r\n     2\t\r\n     3\tWritten 2025-02-14 by the data platform for the warehouse and its nightly loaders as they were on that date, and not\r\n     4\tupdated since. The scheduler's own records of every job run and transaction, later changes included, are in each\r\n     5\textract's `_ops/` folder (below).\r\n     6\t\r\n     7\tThe seven tables below are the warehouse tables that the live scoring service (`live/features.py`) and the churn\r\n     8\tpipeline (`churn/`) read. This page says what each table holds and which system writes it. Every brand of the company\r\n"]
[14.527344, "o", "     9\truns the same warehouse and jobs; each brand's job runs, failures and changes are its own.\r\n    10\t\r\n    11\t## Reading the warehouse\r\n    12\t- A query that starts at a moment t sees exactly the rows and values committed before t: a row committed at or after\r\n    13\t  t is not seen, and a value overwritten at or after t is seen as it was before the overwrite, whatever the date of\r\n    14\t  the event the row records.\r\n    15\t- A query reads each table by the name it has when the query starts.\r\n    16\t- `_loaded_at` is the commit time of the load that wrote the row; in `orders`, `_load_job` names that load.\r\n    17\t- An extract is a copy of every table as the warehouse held it at one moment: the rows and values committed before\r\n    18\t  that moment, with the `_ops/` records written before it.\r\n    19\t- Timestamps are UTC, written as ISO 8601 with a `Z` suffix and whole seconds (`2025-02-04T06:00:00Z`);\r\n    20\t  `snapshot_date` is a UTC date (`YYYY-MM-DD`); amounts are integer cents. Columns whose names "]
[14.527417, "o", "start with `_` are\r\n    21\t  written by the loaders, not by the source systems.\r\n    22\t\r\n    23\t## The scheduler's records (`_ops/`)\r\n    24\t- `_ops/scheduler.log` has one UTC event per line, `<time> <job> run=<run> <event>`: each task's start\r\n    25\t  (`state=running`) and end (`state=success` or `state=failed`), and between them the lines the task writes, such as\r\n    26\t  the rows it selected or copied, the transaction it sent (`sent txn <n> to commit`), the statements it ran and the\r\n    27\t  checks it made.\r\n    28\t- `_ops/commits.jsonl` has one JSON object per transaction a scheduled job sent: `txn`, `at`, `outcome` (`commit` or\r\n    29\t  `rollback`), `table` and `rows` (the table the transaction writes rows into and how many; null for schema\r\n    30\t  statements).\r\n    31\t- `_ops/commits.jsonl` is the commit log of the scheduled jobs: a transaction is committed if and only if its outcome\r\n    32\t  is `commit`, and it is committed at its `at`; the scheduler's task states describe tasks, not commits.\r\n"]
[14.527457, "o", "    33\t- Change data capture (`tickets`), the sign-up service and the scoring service write without the scheduler and are not\r\n    34\t  in `_ops/`.\r\n    35\t\r\n    36\t## Tables\r\n    37\t\r\n    38\t### `customers`\r\n    39\t`customer_id, signup_at, plan, postcode_district, last_login_at, _updated_at`: one row per customer.\r\n    40\t- The sign-up service writes the row at `signup_at`: the plan chosen at sign-up (`basic`, `plus` or `family`), the\r\n    41\t  postcode district, an empty `last_login_at`, and `_updated_at` = `signup_at`. `signup_at` and `postcode_district`\r\n    42\t  never change.\r\n    43\t- `crm_sync` reads the CRM at its start. For each customer whose plan or last login in the CRM (the latest login to\r\n    44\t  the app or website before the sync's start) differs from the row, it overwrites `plan` and `last_login_at` with the\r\n    45\t  CRM's values and sets `_updated_at` to the sync's commit time.\r\n    46\t- Only plan changes are logged (`plan_changes`); no history of `last_login_at` is kept.\r\n    47\t\r\n    48\t"]
[14.527468, "o", "### `plan_changes`\r\n    49\t`customer_id, changed_at, old_plan, new_plan, _loaded_at`: one row per plan change that `crm_sync` has applied. Each\r\n    50\tsync writes one row for every change made in the CRM before its start that no earlier sync applied; `changed_at` is\r\n    51\twhen the customer made the change in the CRM, `_loaded_at` the commit of the sync that applied it. A customer's rows\r\n    52\tare every change applied since sign-up, so the earliest row's `old_plan` is the plan chosen at sign-up. When one sync\r\n    53\tapplies several changes of a customer, the plan after that sync is the `new_plan` of the latest of them (by\r\n    54\t`changed_at`), the CRM's plan at the sync's start.\r\n    55\t\r\n    56\t### `scoring_points`\r\n    57\t`customer_id, as_of, trigger, app_minutes_7d`: one row per scoring request, written by the scoring service at the\r\n    58\tmoment of the request, `as_of`. `trigger` is `batch` (the 06:00 run, which scores a customer every 14 days, on the\r\n    59\tcustomer's billing weekday) or `ticket`"]
[14.527521, "o", " (a request the support desk sends when a ticket is opened, for some of the\r\n    60\ttickets, at the ticket's `opened_at`). `app_minutes_7d` is the product-analytics reading that came with the request (the customer's minutes in\r\n    61\tthe app over the last 7 days), empty when the request came without one (analytics export outages, devices offline).\r\n    62\t`(customer_id, as_of)` is unique.\r\n    63\t\r\n    64\t### `orders`\r\n    65\t`order_id, customer_id, ordered_at, kind, amount_cents, _load_job, _loaded_at`. `kind` is `box` (the weekly box,\r\n    66\tcharged by billing at 05:30 on the customer's billing weekday; `ordered_at` is the charge) or `addon` (an extra item,\r\n    67\tordered at any time). Each order exists in the order system, which the loaders read, from its `ordered_at` on.\r\n    68\tThe nightly `legacy_orders` job writes the table: each run selects orders from the order system and sends them to\r\n    69\t`orders` in one transaction.\r\n    70\t\r\n    71\t### `tickets`\r\n    72\t`ticket_id, customer_id, opened_at, t"]
[14.52753, "o", "opic, _loaded_at`: support tickets, written by change data capture from the\r\n    73\tsupport desk 1 to 30 seconds after `opened_at`.\r\n    74\t\r\n    75\t### `status_snapshots`\r\n    76\t`customer_id, snapshot_date, status, _loaded_at`: the billing system's account status (`active`, `paused`,\r\n    77\t`payment_failed` or `cancelled`) at the end of day `snapshot_date`. Billing records a row for a customer on the\r\n    78\tsign-up day, on every day the status changed, and on the first day of every month, up to the cancellation (a\r\n    79\t`cancelled` row is the customer's last); there is at most one row per customer and date.\r\n    80\t`billing_export` writes each day's partition (all the rows dated D) in one transaction.\r\n    81\t\r\n    82\t### `labels`\r\n    83\t`customer_id, as_of, churned_30d, _loaded_at`: the outcome of a prediction (`customer_id` and `as_of` as in\r\n    84\t`scoring_points`): `churned_30d` is 1 if the customer cancelled in `(as_of, as_of + 30 days]`, else 0. The outcome\r\n    85\tbecomes known at the cancellat"]
[14.527575, "o", "ion, or when the 30 days have passed without one.\r\n    86\t`label_job` writes, in one transaction per run, a row for each prediction whose outcome became known before the run\r\n    87\tstarted and that has no row yet.\r\n"]
